# mgmicky.msg - Micky, the beggar outside the gate (mgmicky.ssl).
# 100-107 look and calls, 110-128 floating remarks, 200+ dialogue. One entry per line; no braces in comments.

{100}{}{You see a man in rags, propped against the wall with both arms wrapped around his stomach.}
{101}{}{You see Micky. He is watching your canteen the way other men watch a card game.}
{102}{}{He is gray around the mouth and all bones under the rags. An empty tin cup sits between his feet, upside down, out of honesty.}
{103}{}{There is some color in his face. He is sitting up straight, like a man trying it out.}
{105}{}{Mister! Water? Clean water?}
{106}{}{Lady! Water? Clean water?}
{107}{}{I saw nothing! I never see anything!}

# 110-117: thirsty.
{110}{}{Water... clean water... anybody...}
{111}{}{Don't drink from the puddle. I drank from the puddle.}
{112}{}{Just a mouthful. I'd pay you back. Somehow.}
{113}{}{Robot says the bomb is safe. Robot doesn't have a stomach.}
{114}{}{My insides are trying to be my outsides.}
{115}{}{Stockholm! Got any...? No. He never does.}
{116}{}{I'm not proud. I used to be. It didn't help.}
{117}{}{Everything I swallow comes back with interest.}

# 120-123: he has had a drink today.
{120}{}{It stayed down. It actually stayed down.}
{121}{}{Best drink of my life. Low bar. Still.}
{122}{}{I can feel my legs. I'd forgotten I had them.}
{123}{}{Bless you, stranger. I'd bless you better if I knew how.}

# 125-128: recovering.
{125}{}{I might stand up tomorrow. Don't rush me.}
{126}{}{Walter could use a man who knows what bad water does. I'm an expert.}
{127}{}{Three days and nothing's come back up. I'm thinking of getting a hat.}
{128}{}{Thirsty. But the ordinary kind. I'd missed it.}

# Greetings.
{200}{}{Water. Please. Not from in there; town water costs money and the puddle is free, and the puddle did this to me. Anything clean. I can't keep the bad stuff down any more.}
{201}{}{You came back. People don't, as a rule. Did you bring... no, don't tell me yet. Let me hope for a second.}
{203}{}{Still down. I keep waiting for it to come back up, and it doesn't. I hardly know what to do with myself.}
{204}{}{Look at me, sitting up like a man with a future. I'm not going to ask today. No, that's a lie. I'm asking. It's habit.}
{205}{}{I'm listening. It's about all I have the strength for.}
{206}{}{One more thing.}
{207}{}{Hang on, Micky.}

# Hub options.
{210}{}{Here. A flask of clean water.}
{211}{}{Will a Nuka-Cola do?}
{212}{}{What happened to you?}
{213}{}{Why are you out here and not inside the wall?}
{214}{}{I have nothing for you.}
{215}{}{Get a job. Then buy your own.}
{216}{}{Me thirsty too.}
{217}{}{Here. Wet for you.}
{218}{}{Bye, sad man.}
{219}{}{Here. Fizzy for you.}

# Drinks.
{220}{}{[He drinks it in one go, then holds very still, like a man listening for footsteps.] ...It's staying. Oh, it's staying down. You don't know what you've... Thank you. Thank you.}
{221}{}{[He drinks slowly this time, making it last.] Still the best thing I ever tasted. Keep this up and I'll start believing in people.}
{222}{}{Twice in one day? You'll ruin me for begging. [He tucks it inside his shirt.] For tonight. Thank you.}
{223}{}{[He drinks, wipes his mouth and digs in his rags.] Here. Bottle caps. A trader swore they're money out east. I believed him; that's how I lost my real money. Take them. Go east. Be rich.}
{224}{}{[He stares at the bottle.] It's brown. It fizzes. It's the cleanest thing I've seen all year. [He drinks.] ...I can feel my teeth. Is that good? I'm calling it good.}

# His story.
{230}{}{Came in guarding a caravan. Stayed for a girl, then stayed for the drink. When the money went I took to dipping a cup in the puddle by the bomb. Tastes fine. Tingles. One morning nothing would stay down, and nothing has since.}
{231}{}{They'd let me in. The gate's open to anybody; the robot greets me by name, the tin bastard. But a man who's sick on Moriarty's floor twice gets shown the outside. The wall keeps the wind off, and Stockholm throws me a crust.}

# Nothing to give.
{232}{}{No. No, of course. It's all right. Go on in. Mind the puddle.}
{233}{}{Then just your company. That's more than most leave.}
{234}{}{A job. I had one. I guarded caravans until my guts quit. You want to hire a man who can't hold water, I'm right here. [He turns his face to the wall.]}
{235}{}{[Leave him.]}

# Low Intelligence.
{236}{}{You too, friend? Then sit. We'll be thirsty together. It's the one thing out here there's plenty of.}
